const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Physician Manuscript Writing Service","serviceType":"Clinical manuscript writing, statistical analysis and journal submission support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/physician-manuscripts/","description":"Manuscript writing for clinicians: the article type settled from your data, written to CONSORT 2025, STROBE, STARD, PRISMA or CARE as the design requires.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What should be included in a physician-authored research manuscript?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Structurally: a title, a structured abstract to the journal’s own headings, keywords, then introduction, methods, results and discussion, with references, tables, figures and declarations around them. But the structure is the easy half. What decides whether the paper can be judged is the reporting guideline the design falls under, because that determines what each section is responsible for — CONSORT 2025 for a randomised trial, STROBE for cohort, case-control and cross-sectional studies, STARD for diagnostic accuracy, TRIPOD+AI for prediction models, PRISMA 2020 for systematic reviews, CARE for case reports, SQUIRE 2.0 for quality improvement. Take the current checklist from the EQUATOR Network, write to it from the outline stage rather than checking against it at the end, and complete it honestly. The gaps it exposes are exactly the ones a reviewer will find, and finding them yourself costs a paragraph where finding them at review costs three months."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know which type of manuscript my data makes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Three questions settle it most of the time. Is there a denominator — a defined population the cases came out of? If yes, you have a rate rather than a description, and that is a study rather than a series. Did you do something to the patients, and if so was the allocation random? That separates a trial from an observational study and decides between CONSORT and STROBE. And are you comparing groups, or describing one? A comparison brings a whole additional set of reviewer questions about how the groups were formed and whether they were comparable. The router at the top of this page walks through those questions and a few more. The mistake we see most often is not a hard call between two types; it is a type that was decided in the first ten minutes and never revisited, usually because the last paper from the department was a series."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the authorship criteria for physician research publications?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ICMJE sets four and all four must be met. Substantial contributions to the conception or design of the work, or to the acquisition, analysis or interpretation of data; drafting the work or reviewing it critically for important intellectual content; final approval of the version to be published; and agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work, including investigating and resolving questions about its accuracy or integrity. Three practical consequences follow. Collecting data without contributing to design, analysis or interpretation does not by itself qualify somebody, though it very often should when you look at what they actually did. Reading a draft and approving it does not meet criterion two. And honorary authorship for a department head who contributed nothing fails criterion four in a way that matters: an author is agreeing to be accountable for the whole paper, which is not a courtesy to hand out. Writing assistance, technical editing, language editing and proofreading are listed by the ICMJE as activities that alone do not qualify a contributor, and such contributors should be acknowledged with their specific contribution described."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"If you write my paper, are you the author?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. A Pubrica writer is acknowledged, not authored, and the acknowledgement is what makes the arrangement legitimate rather than what exposes it. Writing assistance does not on its own meet the ICMJE criteria; the ICMJE says such contributors should be acknowledged with their contribution described, and advises editors to require the corresponding author to obtain written permission from everybody acknowledged — so we supply that permission with the wording rather than leaving you to ask. The ICMJE also requires disclosure of any sponsor’s role in writing the report, which is why the acknowledgement names who paid for the assistance. Where a Pubrica contributor genuinely meets all four criteria — which happens most often on the statistical analysis — we say so and let you decide; nothing bars a writer from being an author, it is the contribution that decides. What we will not do is draft a manuscript for somebody who has not read it, because criterion four is a real undertaking."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you turn my thesis chapter into a journal article?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is a rewrite rather than a trim, which is the part that surprises people. A thesis chapter is written to demonstrate competence to an examiner: the literature review is exhaustive because breadth is being assessed, the methods narrate what you learned as you went, and the discussion covers everything. A paper is written to convince a reviewer of one claim inside a word limit, so the literature review becomes two paragraphs that establish a gap, the methods become a reproducible account with the false starts removed, and the discussion argues a single point. It is also common that one chapter contains two papers, or that two chapters make one, and deciding that is the first conversation. Two practical points. Check your university’s policy on publishing from a deposited thesis, because some repositories make the chapter publicly available and a journal may treat that as prior publication. And settle authorship with your supervisor in writing before drafting rather than after. Note that this is different from writing a thesis for submission, which we do not do — see the question below."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you write my thesis or dissertation for me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. We do not write theses, dissertations or any other assessed work to be submitted as a student’s own. In England, providing, arranging or advertising such a service is a criminal offence under sections 27 and 28 of the Skills and Post-16 Education Act 2022. In Australia, providing or advertising academic cheating services is an offence under the TEQSA Act as amended in 2020, carrying penalties of up to 500 penalty units and two years’ imprisonment. Universities’ academic integrity regulations prohibit a third party completing assessed work and typically permit only limited editing and proofreading within declared boundaries. What we do instead is work inside those boundaries: language editing and proofreading where your institution permits it and to the extent it permits, statistical analysis and its interpretation as a named collaborator, and conversion of an already-examined thesis chapter into a journal article, which is a publication rather than an assessment. If you are unsure where your institution draws the line, ask your supervisor or research office before you ask us — and we will ask you what the answer was."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can a research team standardise its manuscript workflow?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Five things, in order of how much trouble each one prevents. Decide the article type and the reporting guideline before drafting, and record both — this is the decision that costs a rewrite if it is wrong. Write the author list down at the start against the four ICMJE criteria, with a rule for what happens when contributions change. Agree a framework before prose, mapping every section to the checklist item it answers, so a section moving costs a minute rather than breaking every cross-reference. Have somebody who did not write the draft read it against the design, because authors cannot see their own claims. And keep one reference library per project rather than per author, because merging three libraries at submission is where the wrong-year citations come from. For departments producing several papers a year we set this up once as a written house workflow, with templates, and it is usually the cheapest thing we do."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you guarantee publication in a high-impact journal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and neither can anybody else. Acceptance is an editor’s decision informed by reviewers, and a supplier who promises it is selling you something they do not control. We will also not offer “unlimited revisions until acceptance”, which is the same promise wearing a different coat: it ties a fixed fee to an outcome outside the contract, and it is unenforceable in the direction that matters to you. What we do instead is commit to what we can actually deliver — a full year of uncapped revision inside the agreed scope from the date of delivery, on every tier. Within that year a reviewer asking for a reanalysis, a journal changing its format or a co-author joining late are all included. New work is not: a different study, a second paper from the same dataset, or a rewrite for a journal in another field. We say which side of that line something falls on before starting rather than after."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My paper was rejected. Is it worth revising or should I send it elsewhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Read the letter for which of three things it says, because they have different answers. A desk rejection on scope means the paper was never read: send it elsewhere, unchanged, and spend the time choosing better. A rejection with substantive reviewer comments is the most valuable document you will receive about this paper, and sending it elsewhere without addressing them is the commonest expensive mistake in publishing — specialty reviewer pools are small, and the same person may well read it again at the next journal. A rejection that questions the design or the analysis is the one to take seriously: sometimes there is a fix, sometimes the honest answer is a shorter paper making a smaller claim, and occasionally it is that the study cannot support a paper at all. We will read the letter with you and say which of the three it is, including when the answer is the one nobody wants."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Physician manuscripts","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/physician-manuscripts/"}]}];

